FROM tomcat:9-jdk17

COPY target/*.war /usr/local/tomcat/webapps/ROOT.war

EXPOSE 8080

CMD ["catalina.sh", "run"]




Absolutely. For your Online Ticketing System (Maven WAR + Tomcat), use this Dockerfile and keep all Docker commands in the Eclipse Terminal.

1. Dockerfile

Create the file named exactly:

Dockerfile

Place it in the project root, where pom.xml and target are present:

E-ticketing/
├── pom.xml
├── Dockerfile
├── src/
└── target/
    └── LibraryRegistration.war

Write:

FROM tomcat:9-jdk17

COPY target/*.war /usr/local/tomcat/webapps/ROOT.war

EXPOSE 8080

CMD ["catalina.sh", "run"]

What each line means

Dockerfile line	Meaning

FROM tomcat:9-jdk17	Uses Tomcat 9 with JDK 17
COPY target/*.war ...	Copies your Maven WAR into Tomcat
EXPOSE 8080	Container application uses port 8080
CMD ["catalina.sh", "run"]	Starts Tomcat when container starts



---

2. Maven commands before Docker

Go to your project:

cd ~/E-ticketing

Build the WAR:

mvn clean package

Check that the WAR exists:

ls target

You should see:

LibraryRegistration.war


---

3. Docker basic commands — important for viva

Check Docker version

docker --version

Check Docker information

docker info

Login to DockerHub

docker login

Logout

docker logout


---

4. Build Docker image

Because Docker image repository names must be lowercase:

docker build -t ots .

Here:

docker build → builds image

-t ots → gives image the name ots

. → uses current directory as build context


Check images:

docker images

or:

docker image ls


---

5. Tag image for DockerHub

Your DockerHub username is rekha123.

docker tag ots rekha123/ots:latest

Check:

docker images

You should see:

ots
rekha123/ots


---

6. Push image to DockerHub

docker push rekha123/ots:latest

This uploads your image to DockerHub.


---

7. Run the Docker container

First, make sure local Tomcat is stopped, otherwise port 8080 may already be occupied.

Then:

docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name ots-container rekha123/ots:latest

Meaning of -p

-p 8080:8080
   ↑     ↑
 host   container

So:

localhost:8080 → Docker container:8080


---

8. Check running containers

docker ps

You should see:

ots-container

with:

0.0.0.0:8080->8080/tcp


---

9. Check all containers

Even stopped containers:

docker ps -a

This is especially useful if the container immediately stops.


---

10. Check container logs

docker logs ots-container

For continuously watching logs:

docker logs -f ots-container

Press:

Ctrl + C

to stop watching logs.


---

11. Open the application

After:

docker ps

shows the container running, open:

http://localhost:8080

Because we copied the WAR as:

ROOT.war

you don't need:

/LibraryRegistration


---

12. Stop container

docker stop ots-container


---

13. Start an existing stopped container

docker start ots-container


---

14. Restart container

docker restart ots-container


---

15. Remove container

First stop it:

docker stop ots-container

Then:

docker rm ots-container

Or force remove:

docker rm -f ots-container


---

16. Remove image

docker rmi ots

For the DockerHub-tagged image:

docker rmi rekha123/ots:latest


---

17. Pull image from DockerHub

If another developer wants your image:

docker pull rekha123/ots:latest

Then run:

docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name ots-container rekha123/ots:latest


---

